 was required to prove following essential ingredients under section 138.

(a)That a cheque was drawn by the accused;

(b)The cheque was presented by the payee or holder in due course for encashment;

(c)The cheque was returned unpaid by the drawee bank;

(d)That a notice of demand was issued in writing to the drawer calling upon him to pay the cheque amount;

(e)Failure of the drawer to make the payment within 15 days of the receipt of demand notice. Thus, in order to bring home the guilt of the respondents under section 138, the petitioners were first required to establish that the instruments which were presented for encashment and returned unpaid were actually the cheques as defined under section 6.

From section 6, it is clear that a cheque is a bill of exchange drawn on a specified banker authorizing him to pay the amount mentioned in the cheque on demand to the drawee and debit the same to the account of the drawer.

DW-1 deposed on the basis of the record of the bank account maintained in the bank by respondent No. 1-company. He deposed that as per the instructions of respondent No. 1-company, mode of operation of its bank account was joint by two directors of the company, namely respondent No. 2 and petitioner. He further stated that on 31-3-2005 petitioner tendered his resignation as director of the company and vide instructions letter dated 1-4-2005 of respondent No. 1-company, the mode of operation of the bank account of respondent No. 1 was changed inasmuch as that the petitioner was replaced as a joint signatory for operation of bank account by respondent No. 3. Admittedly, the purported cheques on the basis of which complaints had been filed by the petitioner were signed by only one of the joint signatories namely respondent No. 2. Since the aforesaid purported cheques did not bear the signatures of the second joint signatory, the said instruments did not qualify the definition of a valid cheque which could be acted upon by the bank in terms of the instructions regarding the mode of operation of the bank account. Thus, the Metropolitan Magistrate had rightly concluded that aforesaid instruments could not be termed as validly drawn cheques and one of the most essential ingredients of offence under section 138 was lacking in the instant case. Thus, he had rightly acquitted the respondents for the offence under section 138.

As regards the submission of the petitioner that the petitioner could not have known about internal arrangements about mode of operation of bank account of respondent No. 1-company, it was suffice to say that if respondent Nos. 2 and 3 had induced the petitioner to accept the cheques signed by one of the joint signatories only by misrepresenting and concealing the fact that valid cheques could be signed jointly by both the authorized signatories, the respondents might have committed an offence of cheating punishable under section 420 IPC but by no means it could be said that they were guilty of offence under section 138.

Therefore, there was no merit in the petitions for leave to appeal. The petitions were, accordingly, to be dismissed.

R.K. Thakur for the Petitioner. Aditya Gaur for the Respondent.

